$\begin{align}
a^0\cdot a^6&=a^{0+6}
\\\\
&=a^{6}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $x^m\cdot x^n=x^{m+n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
a^0\cdot a^6&=\underbrace{1}_{a^0}\cdot\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a}_\text{6 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a\cdot a}_\text{6 times}
\\\\
&=a^{6}
\end{align}$
